<description>
Swap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.
- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.

Rule:
- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.
- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.
- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.

Key insight:
- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single marker pixels that have exactly one marker on the opposite side of the same row or column through the largest foreground-color barrier",
  "path": "move along that row or column across the contiguous barrier segment",
  "dst": "the background cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the barrier segment",
  "trajectory": "paint only the final adjacent cell on each side with the opposite marker color",
  "transform": "erase the original paired marker cells; leave the barrier and unpaired markers unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 12 rows x 12-14 cols, bg=0
  barrier:
    color: unique most-frequent non-bg color
    orientation: horizontal (spans all/nearly-all cols, 3 rows thick) or vertical (spans all rows, 3 cols wide)
    profile: varying width 1-3 per cross-section; has a spine (1 full-span row or col) with wider bumps (width 2-3) on some lines
    connectivity: usually 1 component; irregular shapes allowed if per-line segments stay contiguous
    pixel_count: 15-26
  markers:
    colors: 2-3 distinct non-bg non-barrier colors; each color lives entirely on one side of the barrier
    side_distribution: 1 color on side A, 1-2 colors on side B [observed]
    total_pixels: 11-16
    each_pixel: single isolated cell (no multi-cell sprites); placed at distance >=2 from nearest barrier cell along that line
  pairing:
    scan: each row (if vertical barrier) or column (if horizontal barrier) independently
    condition: line crosses exactly 1 contiguous barrier segment, not touching grid edge, with exactly 1 marker on each side
    paired_count: 2-6 paired lines per grid; always different colors across the pair
    unpaired_markers: 3-6 markers on one side only; they stay in place unchanged
  transform:
    each paired marker erased from original position and placed at cell immediately adjacent to the opposite end of that line's barrier segment
    barrier unchanged; unpaired markers unchanged; output same size

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid 12xW (W=12-14), bg=0, pick barrier color and 2-3 marker colors
    2. Choose orientation (H or V); build barrier spine spanning full grid in that dimension
    3. Add width-varying bumps (1-3) along the spine to create an irregular cross-section profile; ensure each cross-section line has at most 1 contiguous segment
    4. Build the OUTPUT state — for each line to be paired, place two different-color markers at cells immediately adjacent to opposite ends of the barrier segment
    5. Add unpaired markers on random lines (one side only, distance >=2 from barrier)
    6. Derive INPUT by swapping each paired marker to a random position on the opposite side (distance >=2), keeping unpaired markers in place
    7. Validate: each line has <=1 marker per side; no marker occupies a destination cell of another pair; barrier is most-frequent non-bg color
```
